          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>According to both ICD-11 and DSM-5, which pair of symptoms are considered the 'anchor symptoms' for diagnosing a major depressive episode?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Insomnia and fatigue</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Insomnia and fatigue are common associated features of depression but are not the anchor symptoms. They also occur in many other medical and psychiatric conditions.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Persistently depressed mood and anhedonia</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. Both ICD-11 and DSM-5 identify persistently depressed mood (most of the day, nearly every day) and markedly diminished interest or pleasure — termed anhedonia — as the two core anchor symptoms for diagnosis.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Weight loss and psychomotor agitation</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Weight changes and psychomotor disturbance are associated neurovegetative symptoms, not anchor criteria. A patient may meet diagnostic criteria for depression without either of these.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Feelings of worthlessness and suicidal ideation</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Worthlessness and suicidal ideation are important cognitive symptoms to assess — especially for risk stratification — but they are associated features, not the two anchor criteria required for diagnosis.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "The anchor symptoms are persistently depressed mood and anhedonia (loss of interest or pleasure). Anhedonia is one of the most clinically reliable signs distinguishing a true depressive episode from transient low mood."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "The two anchor symptoms — persistently depressed mood and anhedonia — must be present for at least two weeks, represent a change from previous functioning, and cause clinically significant impairment to meet diagnostic criteria."
                }
              ],
