          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>A 30-year-old man presents to your PHC in acute mania — he has not slept in thre</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A 30-year-old man presents to your PHC in acute mania — he has not slept in three days, is grandiose, and has been spending money recklessly. As the primary-care physician, what is your role?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Initiate long-term lithium prophylaxis and monitor serum levels</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Long-term mood-stabiliser therapy requires specialist supervision with regular monitoring of serum levels, renal function, and thyroid function. This exceeds the primary-care physician's scope in acute mania.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Safely bridge the patient from crisis to specialist care using a Safety–Stabilise–Refer framework</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. The primary-care physician assesses immediate risk behaviours, enlists the family as a safety net, stabilises acutely, and then refers for definitive specialist-supervised management of bipolar disorder.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Prescribe an SSRI to treat the underlying mood disturbance</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>SSRIs are first-line for unipolar depression, not mania. Giving an antidepressant without a mood stabiliser in bipolar disorder risks worsening the manic episode or precipitating rapid cycling.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Discharge with a referral letter for outpatient psychiatry</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Acute mania with reckless behaviour, grandiosity, and sleep deprivation is a psychiatric emergency. Discharging without acute stabilisation and safety measures places the patient and others at significant risk.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "In acute mania, the primary-care physician follows a three-step framework: first assess safety and enlist the family, then stabilise acutely, then refer for definitive specialist management. Long-term bipolar management requires a psychiatrist."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "The three-step framework — Safety, Stabilise, Refer — defines the primary-care physician's role in acute mania: safely bridge the patient from crisis to specialist care rather than independently managing bipolar disorder long-term."
                }
              ],
